(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-diuretics-loop-quiz"]={"title":"The Nephron & Loop Diuretics Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which two hormones act on the collecting duct?","choices":["Parathyroid hormone and calcitonin","Renin and angiotensin II","Aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone","Insulin and glucagon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone act on the collecting duct, controlling sodium and potassium handling and the water channels that decide how concentrated the urine is. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which reflex changes follow the volume depletion caused by a loop diuretic?","choices":["Decreased renin, aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone","Increased renin, aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone","Increased renin with decreased antidiuretic hormone","Decreased renin with increased aldosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Volume depletion drives a reflex rise in the renin-angiotensin system, aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone, which is why the kidney resists the diuretic. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which two effects on uric acid explain why loop diuretics cause gout?","choices":["More urate excretion and a diluted plasma level","Less urate excretion and a concentrated plasma level","More urate production and a diluted plasma level","Less urate production and a concentrated plasma level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics increase proximal reabsorption and decrease tubular excretion of uric acid, and volume contraction concentrates the plasma level. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which agent is a loop diuretic?","choices":["Metolazone","Bumetanide","Triamterene","Methazolamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bumetanide (Bumex) is one of the four loop diuretics, along with furosemide, torsemide and ethacrynic acid. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which acid-base disturbance do loop diuretics cause?","choices":["Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Volume depletion and enhanced hydrogen ion secretion produce a mild metabolic alkalosis, called a contraction alkalosis. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which patient history calls for caution with a loop diuretic because it can precipitate flares?","choices":["Gout","Pulmonary edema","Cirrhosis with ascites","Nephrotic syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics reduce uric acid excretion and concentrate it as volume contracts, so they can precipitate gout flares. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Edema from which of these conditions is treated with a loop diuretic?","choices":["Acute bacterial cystitis","Adrenal insufficiency","Diabetes insipidus","Nephrotic syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In nephrotic syndrome protein loss prevents regulation of plasma fluids, and loop diuretics remove the excess fluid. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which loop diuretic complication can lead to seizures?","choices":["Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyponatremia","Hypermagnesemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Losing too much sodium can cause hyponatremia, which can lead to seizures. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which segment moves weak acids and weak bases into the tubular lumen?","choices":["Collecting duct","Distal convoluted tubule","Descending limb of the loop of Henle","Proximal tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Weak acids and bases are excreted into the lumen at the proximal tubule, where most of the filtrate is also reabsorbed. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of these is an indication for a loop diuretic?","choices":["Volume depletion","Ascites from cirrhosis","Hypokalemia","Contraction alkalosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cirrhosis of the liver with ascites is a listed indication for loop diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which segment reabsorbs about a quarter of the sodium and is impermeable to water?","choices":["Ascending limb of the loop of Henle","Descending limb of the loop of Henle","Proximal tubule","Collecting duct"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ascending limb reabsorbs about 25 percent of the sodium and is impermeable to water, so the sodium leaves without water following. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In which segment is most of the filtrate reabsorbed?","choices":["Loop of Henle","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Proximal tubule"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The proximal tubule reabsorbs about 60 to 70 percent of the filtrate, which is why little of the filtered volume ends up as urine. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the concern when a patient taking lithium for bipolar disorder starts a loop diuretic?","choices":["Faster lithium clearance and loss of effect","Reduced lithium clearance and toxicity","Reduced diuretic effect of the loop agent","Lithium-induced hypokalemia becoming fatal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics decrease lithium clearance and increase its toxicity. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What do all diuretics have in common?","choices":["They increase sodium chloride excretion","They lower the glomerular filtration rate","They raise plasma sodium concentration","They block aldosterone receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diuretics are defined as drugs that increase urine flow and sodium and chloride excretion, and they differ only in where along the nephron they act. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which loop diuretic is marketed under the brand name Lasix?","choices":["Bumetanide","Furosemide","Torsemide","Ethacrynic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Furosemide is sold as Lasix; the other loop diuretics are bumetanide (Bumex), torsemide (Demadex) and ethacrynic acid (Edecrin). — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What happens to water in the descending limb of the loop of Henle?","choices":["It stays in the lumen because the limb is impermeable","It enters the lumen from the interstitium","It leaves the lumen","It follows actively transported sodium out of the lumen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Water leaves the lumen in the descending limb, which helps concentrate the urine; the ascending limb is the water-impermeable part. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A patient taking a loop diuretic is dizzy on standing and has dry mucous membranes. Which adverse effect is most likely?","choices":["Hyperkalemia","Hypermagnesemia","Volume depletion","Fluid overload"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Too much sodium and water loss leaves the patient volume depleted, with orthostatic dizziness and dry mucous membranes. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which antibiotic class adds to the ototoxicity of loop diuretics?","choices":["Penicillins","Cephalosporins","Monobactams","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides potentiate the ototoxicity of loop diuretics, so the combination raises the risk of hearing damage. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which lipid change can loop diuretics cause through sympathetic activation?","choices":["Higher triglycerides","Lower triglycerides","Lower low-density lipoprotein cholesterol","Higher high-density lipoprotein cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Increased sympathetic activity raises triglycerides, a mild hyperlipidemia with loop diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which hormone rises as part of the compensation that blunts a diuretic and works to hold on to water?","choices":["Atrial natriuretic peptide","Parathyroid hormone","Antidiuretic hormone","Glucagon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antidiuretic hormone rises along with renin and aldosterone as part of diuretic braking, and it retains water. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which laboratory value is the diabetes-specific concern when a patient with diabetes starts a loop diuretic?","choices":["Platelet count","Serum bilirubin","Serum amylase","Blood glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics have diabetogenic effects, so blood glucose can rise and should be monitored. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which transport protein do loop diuretics inhibit?","choices":["Sodium-potassium-2-chloride carrier","The sodium-chloride transporter","The luminal sodium channel","The sodium-hydrogen exchanger"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics inhibit the sodium-potassium-2-chloride carrier on the luminal membrane of the thick ascending limb, trapping those ions in the urine. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
